<article class="env-lemma" id="0FAE">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0FAE">Lemma <span data-tag="0FAE">42.46.9</span>.</a> In Situation <a href="/tag/02QL" data-tag="02QL">42.7.1</a> let $X$ be locally of finite type over $S$. Let $E \in D(\mathcal{O}_ X)$ be a perfect object whose Chern classes are defined. Then $c_ i(E^\vee ) = (-1)^ i c_ i(E)$, $P_ i(E^\vee ) = (-1)^ iP_ i(E)$, and $ch_ i(E^\vee ) = (-1)^ ich_ i(E)$ in $A^ i(X)$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      First proof: argue as in the proof of Lemma <a href="/tag/0FAC" data-tag="0FAC">42.46.6</a> to reduce to the case where $E$ is represented by a bounded complex of finite locally free modules of fixed rank and apply Lemma <a href="/tag/0FA5" data-tag="0FA5">42.43.3</a>. Second proof: use the splitting principle discussed in Remark <a href="/tag/0FAD" data-tag="0FAD">42.46.8</a> and use that the chern roots of $E^\vee $ are the negatives of the chern roots of $E$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>